{
  "lesson_summary": "Today's lesson plans to teach students how to compare and order unlike fractions. It will cover two methods: cross multiplication, using the example of Hira's bottle with $\\frac{2}{3}$ and her friend's bottle with $\\frac{3}{5}$, and finding common denominators. The lesson will also discuss how to order three or more fractions.",
  "questions": [
    {
      "index": 1,
      "slo_id": "S1",
      "level": "apply",
      "question": "When comparing $\\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\frac{3}{5}$ using cross multiplication, what is the product of the numerator of the first fraction and the denominator of the second fraction?",
      "options": [
        "10",
        "9",
        "6"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "To find the first cross product, you multiply the numerator of the first fraction (2) by the denominator of the second fraction (5), which equals 10.",
      "selected_because": "the cross multiplication example of Hira's bottle",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Multiplies second numerator by first denominator",
        "2": "Multiplies numerators together"
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "That's right! $2 \\times 5 = 10$. This is the first step in cross multiplication to compare fractions.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "This is the product of the second numerator and the first denominator. Remember, for the first fraction, you multiply its numerator by the other fraction's denominator. So, $2 \\times 5 = 10$.",
          "2": "This is the product of the two numerators. When cross multiplying $\\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\frac{3}{5}$, the first step is to multiply the numerator of the first fraction (2) by the denominator of the second fraction (5), which is 10."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null
    }
  ]
}
